WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:06.480
In school, I learned the trigonometric functions&nbsp;
kinda like this. You have a right angled triangle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.060
an angle typically called theta here,&nbsp;
mathematicians love greek letters, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.840
label the sides, opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse.&nbsp;
Then you have the famous formulas, sine equals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:26.100
opposite over hypotenuse, cosine equals adjacent&nbsp;
over hypotenuse, and tan = opposite over adjacent.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.000
In 20 years of development, even in graphics&nbsp;
work, I think for me this triangle has come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.900
in handy close to zero times, so instead, another&nbsp;
way of visualising this is to start with a circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:45.540
and to simplify things further we use&nbsp;
one with a radius of exactly 1. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.700
any point that lies here on the circle, is&nbsp;
at a distance of exactly 1 from the centre.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:56.880
Typically, this angle here between our vector&nbsp;
and the x axis, theta, is expressed in degrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:04.200
ranging from 0 to 360, but apparently this&nbsp;
is some arbitrary system leftover from, well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.760
they’re not quite sure, but if you think about&nbsp;
the circumference of a circle, that’s defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:17.520
by 2xPIxR, with a radius of 1, that boils down to&nbsp;
just 2xPI, so instead of degrees, if we started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.000
at the x axis here, and moved 1 unit of distance&nbsp;
along the circle, we could invent new unit for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.400
angles, called a radian, so now we express angles&nbsp;
in terms of distance along the outside of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:36.120
circle, and thus a circle is 2 PI radians, instead&nbsp;
of 360 degrees, and that's how radians are made.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:41.940
Now, given this point here on the circle, it&nbsp;
forms an angle theta, and since the radius of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:48.420
this circle is 1, the hypotenuse is 1, so sin&nbsp;
is just this distance here along the vertical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.920
and that means cosine is just this&nbsp;
distance here along the horizontal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:02:00.720
and tan, well if you extend a line here from x&nbsp;
= 1 upwards, and you extend the vector forming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.720
your hypotenuse out this way, where they&nbsp;
2 meet, tan gives you this distance here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.300
the distance from their intersection down to&nbsp;
the x axis. I never use this, but it’s there.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.940
As you move this point around the circle,&nbsp;
we obviously expect cos theta, sin theta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.060
and tan theta to change, and&nbsp;
we can graph those changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:26.880
so sin theta oscillates between 1 and&nbsp;
-1 as this vector rotates the circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:33.060
and cos theta oscillates in much the same way,&nbsp;
but just slightly offset from sin of theta.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:40.740
Tan is interesting, because at PI/2 and -PI/2,&nbsp;
you can see that tan theta is undefined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.520
hopefully now seeing this in&nbsp;
action an what tan really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:50.040
the graph of tan theta makes more sense,&nbsp;
why it kinda freaks out at certain values.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.240
This is where it gets a little&nbsp;
interesting. If we call this vector A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.180
we know cos theta is just the x component&nbsp;
of this vector here on the unit circle.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.060
Or, you could say this a different way, a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:10.500
longer way of saying it is in terms of&nbsp;
this vector A, it is a.x * 1 + a.y * 0

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.920
Or again, now we can say it&nbsp;
in terms of both A and B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.180
where you know the B vector&nbsp;
is just the x axis, or (1, 0)

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.840
Sso it’s a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.580
Which is, now defined in a more mathematical&nbsp;
way, it has a name, it’s the dot product. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:36.240
the dot product of a and b = cos theta, which is&nbsp;
just this horizontal distance here, if a &amp; b are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:44.400
unit vectors, the more general equation you were&nbsp;
taught in school was a.b = ||a|| ||b|| cos theta.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:51.000
So now it might make more intuitive sense&nbsp;
then for why, given any 2 unit vectors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.900
if we take the dot product&nbsp;
between them, you get cos theta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.180
because if you were to rotate these things&nbsp;
around the circle until we hit the x axis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.680
and suddenly this is just reduced to&nbsp;
our simple distance along the x axis

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.060
Actual real proofs exist,&nbsp;
with real math and everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.660
this isn't one of them, just a&nbsp;
nice way of visualising things.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.640
But this is also why the dot product is&nbsp;
used to get the scalar projection of 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.000
vector onto another, because&nbsp;
that’s all this distance is.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.100
So then, how are these functions&nbsp;
implemented? If you have a chunk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.240
code, when you call your hardware or&nbsp;
library function for stuff like sin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:36.180
what kind of math sorcery are they doing? Well,&nbsp;
these tend to be computed in different ways.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.460
Various approximations have existed&nbsp;
for ages. Back in the 7th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:47.280
the Indian mathematician Bhaskara gave the&nbsp;
following formula, which I’m not going to read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.660
but it worked really well in the range of [0,&nbsp;
PI], giving an absolute error of less than 0.0016.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.880
In game development, way back in the&nbsp;
day, it was often acceptable to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.020
something called a lookup table.&nbsp;
By precomputing values and either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:08.400
finding the closest match or linearly&nbsp;
interpolating between adjacent values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.020
it was possible to use a relatively small&nbsp;
lookup table to get acceptable results.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:22.020
A lookup table with 512 entries might&nbsp;
have an error of around 0.000001, or 1e-6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.340
but getting things super right&nbsp;
in games isn’t usually critical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.480
nobody’s life is on the line, unless&nbsp;
you’re jigsaw, so for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.540
you could use 16 values from 0 to pi/2&nbsp;
and that gives an error of around 0.0014,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.880
but cache misses are slow these days, so&nbsp;
lookup tables aren't used as often anymore

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:47.820
If you were to compute these trig functions&nbsp;
directly, the first thought is a taylor expansion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:53.220
and courtesy of wikipedia, they look&nbsp;
like this, which does the job nicely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:57.300
but a common complaint with taylor&nbsp;
series is that the error isn’t uniform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.940
the error scales the further you are from 0.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:05.400
Better approaches have a general theme. They&nbsp;
combine a whole bunch of ideas. They tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:10.680
do what’s called a range reduction, followed by&nbsp;
an approximation, and then a reconstruction step.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.700
First off, looking at a sin wave,&nbsp;
you clearly don’t need to approximate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:20.220
from -infinity to infinity, we already&nbsp;
know that it repeats after [0, 2 PI].

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.260
From there, you can exploit symmetry,&nbsp;
this half is really the same as this half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:30.600
just upside down, and this half of the wave&nbsp;
is really just a mirror of the other side.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.340
So in reality, you only need to&nbsp;
approximate a small portion. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.780
now you have 4 identical quadrants for&nbsp;
this function, just flipped and reversed.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:44.940
So given a point in any of these quadrants,&nbsp;
you’d map it to a point in the first. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.960
you may use a polynomial approximation&nbsp;
like the taylor series we just covered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:55.080
or something more advanced like minimax, anyway,&nbsp;
you compute your value, then you map it back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.360
the appropriate quadrant, and voila, you have an&nbsp;
idea of how your hardware computes a sin value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:04.020
The sin function on the screen is&nbsp;
running the code you see on the left.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:09.780
Intel has a paper from like 20 years ago which&nbsp;
details how a lot of their trig functions were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.060
implemented, you can also poke around the gnu&nbsp;
c library and see similar ideas in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.420
if you can read the code, it’s&nbsp;
not the easiest to work through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.820
Robin Green has a nice writeup on their blog&nbsp;
as well, which is linked in the description.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.520
Lastly, this explanation is taken from an&nbsp;
up and coming math course I’m working on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:34.680
It’ll be available soon, if you’re interested&nbsp;
in more intuitive explanations of trigonometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.740
matrices, and math for game&nbsp;
development, check it out.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.600
Until next time,
